  "query_preview": "..The upper air pattern stays progressive over the\nnext 7 days, although each trough that moves through will be\ndry. The first trough moves through the region today into\nSaturday with the core staying mostly to the north. This low\nwill create a cumulus field over most of the CWA later this \nevening into Saturday with maybe a virga shower or two Saturday \nmorning. Cumulus development has already started over western\nPima County. The White Mountains will see a slight chance (10-20\npercent) for light snow and rain as well. Otherwise expect dry \nand cool conditions with highs today around normal and 5-8 \ndegrees below normal Saturday. After the passage of the trough \ndrier air will move in with surface dewpoints dropping from the \nlow 40s to upper 20s. The drier air will also allow low \ntemperatures to drop with some areas east of Tucson seeing \nfreezing temperatures. Because of this a Freeze Watch is in \neffect Sunday morning for the Sulphur Springs Valley, Benson/St.\nDavid area, and southern Greenlee County. The main limiting \nfactor for low temps will be high level cloud cover, which could\nkeep low temps a little warmer.\n\nMonday a shortwave moves through northern Arizona or southern\nUtah. This system looks to be dry with any rain chances well\nnorth of southeast Arizona. The main impact it will have is\nknocking down temperatures a few degrees below normal Monday.\nRidging will then be in place Tuesday and Wednesday and \npotentially beyond Wednesday. Cluster analysis and ensemble \nmeans show continued ridging, but the deterministic GFS has a \ntrough moving through the region late next week. High \ntemperatures after Monday will be around normal to a few degrees\nabove normal with freezing temperatures possible again east of \nTucson Tuesday morning.",
